Purpose: Document verification findings and hazards checked

[49m[38;5;10m+      1[0m:[38;5;10m[49m # Differential verification of `translation/` against `c_src/`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      2[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      3[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Ground truth: `c_src/src/main.c`, built with CMake into `c_src/build/driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      4[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Candidate: `translation/src/main.rs`, built with `cargo build --release` into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/target/release/driver`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Comparison method: both programs are run as subprocesses with identical
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m arguments and the same forced `argv[0]`, and **stdout, stderr and exit status**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are compared byte for byte. See `translation/tests/differential.rs`. The Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m code is never loaded as a library.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No behavioural mismatch was found.** Every enumerated input produced
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical stdout, byte-identical stderr and the same exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The sections below record what was probed and why, because the hazards in this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program are all in places where a plausible Rust translation *would* diverge.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each one is a mismatch that was checked for and did not occur.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Reachable branches in the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` has exactly three rejecting branches and one accepting path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `perform_expensive_operations` is branch-free.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | C condition | Observable behaviour | Test |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 1 | `argc != 2` | `Usage: <argv[0]> <seed>` on stderr, exit 1 | `argc_branch`, `usage_message_echoes_argv0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 2 | `*endptr != '\0'` | `Invalid seed: '<argv[1]>'` on stderr, exit 1 | `trailing_garbage_branch` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 3 | `errno != 0` (ERANGE) | same message, exit 1 | `out_of_range_branch`, `very_long_argument` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 4 | `temp_seed > UINT_MAX` | same message, exit 1 | `out_of_range_branch` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 5 | none of the above | `srand`, 2000 workload passes, `printf("%d\n", xor)`, exit 0 | `accepted_seeds_produce_identical_output` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Branches 2, 3 and 4 are the three disjuncts of a single `||`, and each is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m covered by inputs that trip it while the other two are false. Because all three
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produce the identical message and status, the short-circuit evaluation order is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not observable — no test depends on it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Hazards checked, and the C behaviour that had to be reproduced
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. An empty argument is a *valid* seed, not an error
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtoul("")` performs no conversion, so glibc sets `endptr = nptr`. `*endptr`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is therefore the terminating NUL, `*endptr != '\0'` is **false**, `errno` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m untouched, and `temp_seed` is 0. The program accepts it and runs with seed 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the single most likely place for a translation to diverge: a Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `str::parse::<u64>()` returns `Err` on `""` and would exit 1 where the C exits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0 and prints a number. The translation models `endptr` as an offset and, for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the no-conversion case, resets it to 0 exactly as glibc does — so the check
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m becomes `end != arg.len()`, which is `0 != 0` for an empty argument. Verified:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m both print `42032659` and exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m By contrast `" "` and `"   "` are rejected, because there `endptr = nptr` points
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m at a space, not at the NUL. Both behaviours are tested.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `strtoul` silently wraps negative values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtoul` accepts a leading `-` and negates the magnitude modulo 2^64. So:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"-1"` → `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`, no ERANGE → rejected by `> UINT_MAX`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"-0"` → `0` → **accepted**, seed 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"-18446744073709551615"` → magnitude is exactly `ULONG_MAX`, which does *not*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   overflow, so there is no ERANGE; negating gives `1` → **accepted**, seed 1.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"-18446744069414584321"` → negates to exactly `4294967295` (`UINT_MAX`) →
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   **accepted**. I initially classified this as out-of-range; it is not. One
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   less than that magnitude, `"-18446744069414584320"`, negates to `4294967296`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and *is* rejected. Both are tested, on the sides they actually land on.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"-18446744073709551616"` → magnitude overflows → ERANGE, and glibc returns
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ULONG_MAX` **without** applying the sign → rejected.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A translation that rejected all leading `-` signs, or that applied the sign to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the ERANGE sentinel, would diverge on four of these.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. ERANGE versus the `> UINT_MAX` check are different rejections
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"18446744073709551615"` (`ULONG_MAX`) parses cleanly — no ERANGE — and is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m rejected only by `temp_seed > UINT_MAX`. `"18446744073709551616"` sets ERANGE.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both are tested so that neither check can be dropped without a failure. A
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 100,000-digit argument (`very_long_argument`) covers the ERANGE path at a length
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m where a naive accumulate-and-compare could itself overflow or panic; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation uses `checked_mul`/`checked_add` and latches an overflow flag,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m matching glibc's "consume every digit, return `ULONG_MAX`, set ERANGE".
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. `srand`/`rand` must be glibc's generator, not any PRNG
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The output is a XOR fold over 262,144 values seeded from `rand()`, so any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m deviation in the generator changes the single number on stdout. The translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reimplements glibc's TYPE_3 additive-feedback `random_r` (degree 31,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m separation 3, 310 discarded outputs, low bit dropped), including glibc's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "seed must not be 0" fixup — which is why seed 0 and seed 1 print the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m value (`42032659`). Verified across 40 distinct accepted seeds: `0`, `1`, `7`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `9`, `12`, `2`, `3`, `5`, `33`, `42`, `100`, `777`, `1000`, `1024`, `12345`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `65535`, `65536`, `88888888`, `305419896`, `123456789`, `999999937`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `1000000000`, `1431655765`, `2147483647`, `2147483648`, `2147483649`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `2863311530`, `3000000000`, `4000000000`, `4294967294`, `4294967295`, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign/whitespace/leading-zero spellings of several of those. Seeds above
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `INT_MAX` are included because `srand` takes an `unsigned int`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. The hot loop relies on wrapping, arithmetic shift and C division
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x = x * 3 + 7;        // signed overflow -> must wrap, not panic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x = x ^ (x >> 3);     // must be an arithmetic (sign-propagating) shift
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x = x - (x << 1);     // left shift of a negative int; wraps
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m x = x / 2 + x % 7;    // truncation toward zero; % takes the sign of x
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m In debug Rust, `x * 3 + 7` panics on overflow, and `x % 7` for negative `x` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a common source of Python/Rust confusion. The translation uses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add`/`wrapping_sub`/`wrapping_shl` and relies on `i32`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `>>` being arithmetic and `/` and `%` truncating toward zero as C does. This is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exercised over roughly 40 × 262,144 starting values run through 200,000 update
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m steps each; every one of those runs folds into a value that matched the C.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `x << 1` on a negative `int` is undefined behaviour in C, so this is a case
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m where the C "looks like a bug". Per the instructions it was replicated rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than fixed: the translation reproduces what the compiled C actually does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (two's-complement wrapping), confirmed empirically by the matching output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. `argv[1]` may not be valid UTF-8
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The error message echoes the raw argument. `String::from_utf8_lossy` would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m substitute U+FFFD and diverge. The translation reads arguments as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `OsStr` bytes and writes them straight out. Verified with `\xff`, `\xff\xfe`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `12\xff`, `\xc3` and `\x80\x80`, plus Arabic-Indic digits `\u{661}\u{662}`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (which `strtoul` does not treat as digits).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. `argv[0]` is echoed verbatim, including when `argc == 0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Tested with `argv[0]` set to `driver`, `/weird/path/to/driver`, `x` and `""`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m via `CommandExt::arg0`. Also tested the `argc == 0` case, which no shell can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m produce, using a helper that calls `execve` with an empty `argv`: both programs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m print `Usage:  <seed>` and exit 1. The translation's `argv.first()` /
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `unwrap_or(b"")` matches.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. Exit status and output framing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` returns 0 or 1; the Rust program calls `process::exit` with the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m values. stdout is exactly `%d\n` with no padding or precision. Both are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m compared on every case, so a translation that exited 0 on an error path would
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fail even though its stdout is empty in both.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Notes on the test harness
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two defects were in my test code, not in the translation, and are recorded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m here only so the numbers above are not misread:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - The first exploratory shell loop left `$args` unquoted, so `"   "` was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   word-split away and the case actually tested `argc == 1`. The committed Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   tests pass arguments as an `OsString` vector, which cannot word-split.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `"-18446744069414584321"` was initially listed under both the rejecting and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the accepting test. It belongs only in the accepting one; see §2.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both programs print `argv[0]`, so a naive comparison would always differ on the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m usage path by the two binaries' paths. Rather than normalising the text
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m afterwards, every invocation forces the same `argv[0]` with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `CommandExt::arg0` (the equivalent of `exec -a driver ...`), which keeps the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stderr comparison a genuine byte comparison.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Cost
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `accepted_seeds_produce_identical_output` is slow by nature: one run is 2000
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m passes over 262,144 `int`s with 100 update steps each, about 5 min for the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m binary and about 8 min for the C one. The harness launches every process pair
